 ## 2026-07-20
 
+### 0935 — ReluxNIOSSH adapter blocked by three missing public fork seams (TASK-260715-1af33i)
+- BLOCKER: The neutral credential is an opaque async signer (`SSHPublicKeyCredential`), but ReluxNIOSSH public-key auth accepts only concrete `NIOSSHPrivateKey` and signs synchronously inside an internal message initializer. The adapter cannot authenticate without a candidate downcast/private-key export or a new external-signer fork API.
+- BLOCKER: ReluxNIOSSH exposes only TCP-forwarding as a public global request. Its generic reply-requiring request sender and message type are internal, so the adapter cannot send/measure a supported `keepalive@openssh.com` request. Substituting remote TCP forwarding or writing encrypted packets beside the engine is invalid.
+- BLOCKER: `SSHClientConfiguration` exposes transport-protection schemes but not caller KEX/host-key allowlists or a public exact negotiated KEX/host-key/cipher/MAC result; the only negotiation result/test accessor is internal. Inferring results from capabilities would violate E-ALGO.
+- ROUTING: Product code stopped per TASK-260715-1af33i's explicit insufficient-fork rule. Recommended resume path is a focused TASK-260715-nzdzv3 fork extension for async external signing, generic/specific keepalive reply observation, configurable algorithm allowlists, and an immutable negotiated-algorithm snapshot/event. Full evidence and rejected forced fits are attached as `TASK-260715-1af33i_fork-api-blocker.md`.
+
 ### 0908 — Review: minimal ReluxNIOSSH fork accepted; two adapter-facing caveats (TASK-260715-nzdzv3)
 - MILESTONE: Fork accepted → `done`. Reviewer independently re-ran every gate: `make validate-reluxniossh` (exact archive/license SHA-256, 16-file allowlist, 323 upstream XCTest + 10 Swift Testing, build), strict `swift format lint`, same-pin `conflict-test`, `make validate-core` (49 tests/build) — all exit 0. Default-path equivalence of the new adjust math to upstream verified analytically for even window sizes (default 1<<24); protected-byte boundary matches contract §10.1 (post-encode pre-write at `NIOSSHHandler.swift:266`, post-read pre-decode at `NIOSSHHandler.swift:205`); all outbound frames flow through the single `writeMessage` path.
 - FINDING: During any KEX (client- or server-initiated) upstream's state machine rejects outbound `channelData` with `protocolViolation` (`SSHConnectionStateMachine.swift:1060-1076`), failing the child write promise. Pre-existing upstream behavior, unchanged by the fork — but automatic byte/time rekey makes the collision window likely under bulk traffic. The NIOSSH adapter (TASK-260715-1af33i) MUST gate/queue child writes on `rekeySnapshot.keyExchangeInProgress` / `NIOSSHRekeyStartedEvent`→`NIOSSHRekeySucceededEvent`; the fork exposes exactly this observability for the contract's "writes MAY suspend" clause.

